# Sketch compatibility specification

# Status and authority

This document specifies mm.sketch, the module providing sketch compatibility for modules.cpp applications. It describes implemented behavior for stages 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, and 7 (release v1.2.3). Features scheduled for later stages are explicitly identified as planned.

docs/modules-ino.mdy specifies the mm.ino preprocessor that transforms sketch source files into an application translation unit; docs/modules-c++20.mdy owns the language dialect, include permissions, and linkage rules; docs/modules-platform-mcu.mdy owns the underlying MCU facilities; docs/modules-stdio.mdy owns the platform console interface; docs/modules-execution.mdy owns the execution model and loop scheduling.

# Role

mm.sketch provides a sketch programming model above mm.mcu and mm.stdio. It lets sketches run as standard ISO C++20 applications on any platform supported by modules.cpp.

The module exports the implemented sketch vocabulary in namespace mm::sketch. The generated application prelude introduces using namespace mm::sketch, so sketches access functions, types, and constants without qualification.

# The runner

Opening fence
using Setup = void (*)();
using Loop = void (*)();

int run(Setup setup, Loop loop);
void requestExit(int code = 0);
[[nodiscard]] bool exitRequested();
[[nodiscard]] int exitCode();
Closing fence

run is the application's main entry point, invoked from the generated main. It performs four steps in order:

1. Initialize the console via mm::stdio and record the status, allowing Serial.begin to report the result.
2. Call setup once.
3. Execute the loop: call loop, check exitRequested, and call dispatch if exit has not been requested, repeating until requestExit is called.
4. Return the exit code passed to requestExit (defaulting to 0).

requestExit records the requested status code and raises an exit flag. It does not throw an exception or invoke longjmp; execution continues until the current call returns.

A raised exit flag immediately terminates any active wait. delay and waiting console operations check exitRequested between slices and return early without completing their full duration or timeout. delay returns false, and waiting console operations return whatever count was accumulated.

Cancellation is not an error. An early return caused by exitRequested does not record a failure in the error latch; lastError retains the first actual failure, preserving any subsequent failure diagnostic within that same loop iteration.

run inspects the exit flag after setup returns, before calling dispatch after each loop returns, and after each dispatched interrupt handler completes. If requestExit is called during setup, loop never runs. If called during loop, the remainder of that loop iteration completes, dispatch is skipped, and run returns.

run clears the exit flag and exit code on entry, allowing test suites to invoke run repeatedly within the same process. Console initialization runs on each invocation; the interrupt dispatch table is preserved across runs.

The cancellation function is named requestExit rather than exit to avoid collisions with the standard C library exit function introduced when sketches include headers such as <string> or <cstdlib>.

Because run is invoked directly from main, its execution loop is the application's primary thread of execution. Handlers dispatched between iterations run on the application thread, fulfilling the execution model requirement that dispatch occurs within thread mode.

# LED_BUILTIN and electrical polarity

Opening fence
struct Led {};
inline constexpr Led LED_BUILTIN{};

bool pinMode(Led, Mode mode);
bool digitalWrite(Led, Level level);
[[nodiscard]] Level digitalRead(Led);

bool hasBuiltinLed();
bool ledOn();
bool ledOff();
Closing fence

LED_BUILTIN is not an integer pin number. It is a distinct object of type Led. Overloads of pinMode, digitalWrite, and digitalRead accept Led and resolve the board's configured LED pin dynamically via mm::mcu::board().

Resolving the LED pin at call time ensures that board configuration provided by the platform provider is fully initialized before use. If the target board defines no LED, these overloads return false (or LOW for digitalRead) and latch Status::Unsupported. hasBuiltinLed queries whether the board defines an LED without modifying the error latch.

HIGH and LOW represent electrical voltage levels, not illumination states. digitalWrite(LED_BUILTIN, HIGH) drives the physical GPIO line high. On a board wired with an active-low LED, driving the line high turns the LED off.

To control illumination without regard to board wiring polarity, mm.sketch provides ledOn and ledOff. These functions inspect the board definition's active_high property and drive the pin to the appropriate level to illuminate or extinguish the LED. digitalWrite is electrical; ledOn is illuminating. Sketches ported from active-high boards may use either; sketches targeting active-low boards should use ledOn and ledOff.

# Status and error latching

Opening fence
enum class Status {
    Ok,
    BadArgument,
    Unsupported,
    Busy,
    Timeout,
    TransportError,
    NotInitialized
};

[[nodiscard]] Status lastError();
[[nodiscard]] const char* lastCall();
void clearError();
Closing fence

mm::sketch::Status unifies the status codes returned by mm.mcu and mm.stdio.

In stage 1, sketch functions that perform actions (pinMode, digitalWrite, delay, Serial.begin) return bool, where true indicates success. Functions that return readings (digitalRead, millis) return their value on success, and a defined fallback on failure: LOW or 0. Fallback values are not valid readings; they signify an operation that could not be completed. In stage 3, value-reading Serial operations will return corresponding fallbacks (-1 or 0).

When any sketch operation fails, the module records the resulting Status and the name of the failing function in an internal error latch. lastError and lastCall report the first failure recorded since the latch was last cleared. Subsequent failures do not overwrite earlier ones.

clearError resets the error latch to Status::Ok and empty string. Nothing else clears the latch; neither a subsequent successful call, nor the start of a new loop iteration, nor entry into run will reset it. A sketch wishing to verify an iteration calls clearError at the start of loop and checks lastError at the end.

# Dispatch and latency

Opening fence
void dispatch();
Closing fence

dispatch executes on the sketch thread and performs four steps in order:

1. Drain incoming bytes from the console into an internal receive ring buffer.
2. Snapshot the generation numbers of all eight interrupt slots.
3. Iterate over the slots in index order. For each slot that is active and whose generation matches the initial snapshot, dispatch queries mm.mcu for pending edges via gpio_take. If an edge occurred, the registered handler is invoked on the sketch thread.
4. Invoke the serial callback if unread bytes remain in the ring buffer.

dispatch is guarded against recursion. If a handler or nested call invokes dispatch directly or via delay, the nested dispatch returns immediately. Handlers run at most once per outer dispatch pass.

Modifications to the interrupt table during handler execution take effect safely:
- If a handler detaches itself or another slot, the slot is cleared and skipped during the remainder of the current pass.
- If a handler attaches a new pin, the slot receives a new generation count distinct from the snapshot, deferring execution of the new handler to the next pass.

Handlers are executed strictly in application context at defined dispatch points:
- Between iterations of loop inside run.
- During delay, which sleeps in slices of at most 10 milliseconds and calls dispatch between slices.
- During waiting console operations between polling attempts.
- When explicitly called by the sketch via dispatch.

Handlers never execute inside non-waiting calls such as pinMode, digitalWrite, digitalRead, or Serial.print, and never execute in hardware interrupt context.

# Vocabulary by section

## Digital I/O

Opening fence
enum class Mode {
    Input,
    Output,
    InputPullup,
    InputPulldown
};

inline constexpr Mode INPUT = Mode::Input;
inline constexpr Mode OUTPUT = Mode::Output;
inline constexpr Mode INPUT_PULLUP = Mode::InputPullup;
inline constexpr Mode INPUT_PULLDOWN = Mode::InputPulldown;

enum class Level {
    Low = 0,
    High = 1
};

inline constexpr Level LOW = Level::Low;
inline constexpr Level HIGH = Level::High;

bool pinMode(unsigned int pin, Mode mode);
bool digitalWrite(unsigned int pin, Level level);
[[nodiscard]] Level digitalRead(unsigned int pin);
Closing fence

pinMode configures GPIO direction and internal pull resistors. digitalWrite sets pin voltage. digitalRead queries pin voltage; on failure it returns LOW and records the error status.

## Analog I/O

Opening fence
[[nodiscard]] int analogRead(unsigned int pin);
void analogReadResolution(int bits);

void analogWrite(unsigned int pin, int value);
void analogWrite(Led, int value);
void analogWriteResolution(int bits);
Closing fence

analogRead samples the analog voltage on pin via mm.mcu adc_read. If pin corresponds to an attached ADC channel via adc_channel_for_gpio or matches an ADC channel number directly, the channel is claimed and sampled. The reading is scaled to the resolution configured by analogReadResolution (defaulting to 10 bits, range [0, 1023]). On failure or unsupported platforms, analogRead returns 0 and records the error status.

analogReadResolution sets the resolution of values returned by analogRead in bits (range [1, 31], default 10). Invalid bit values record Status::BadArgument and leave the resolution unchanged.

analogWrite generates a pulse-width modulated (PWM) wave on pin at 490 Hz (period 2,040,816 ns) via mm.mcu pwm_configure and pwm_write. value specifies the duty cycle, scaled by the write resolution (default 8 bits, [0, 255]). A value <= 0 drives steady LOW (duty 0); a value >= max drives steady HIGH (100% duty). The LED_BUILTIN overload resolves the board's configured LED pin via mm::mcu::board(). If the pin or platform does not support PWM, analogWrite records Status::BadArgument or Status::Unsupported.

analogWriteResolution sets the duty cycle resolution for analogWrite in bits (range [1, 31], default 8). Invalid bit values record Status::BadArgument and leave the resolution unchanged.

analogReference is declined; reference voltage configuration is a board property managed through hardware configuration.

## Advanced I/O

Opening fence
enum class BitOrder : byte {
    LsbFirst = 0,
    MsbFirst = 1
};

inline constexpr BitOrder LSBFIRST = BitOrder::LsbFirst;
inline constexpr BitOrder MSBFIRST = BitOrder::MsbFirst;

byte shiftIn(unsigned int data_pin, unsigned int clock_pin, BitOrder bit_order);
void shiftOut(unsigned int data_pin, unsigned int clock_pin, BitOrder bit_order, byte val);

[[nodiscard]] unsigned long pulseIn(unsigned int pin, Level value, unsigned long timeout = 1000000UL);
[[nodiscard]] unsigned long pulseInLong(unsigned int pin, Level value, unsigned long timeout = 1000000UL);

void tone(unsigned int pin, unsigned int frequency, unsigned long duration = 0);
void noTone(unsigned int pin);
Closing fence

shiftIn and shiftOut provide bit-banged synchronous serial communication over digitalWrite and digitalRead. In shiftIn, the clock pin is pulsed HIGH to read each of eight bits from the data pin. In shiftOut, each of eight bits is driven onto the data pin before pulsing the clock pin HIGH and LOW. The bit order is governed by BitOrder: LSBFIRST shifts the least-significant bit first, and MSBFIRST shifts the most-significant bit first.

pulseIn and pulseInLong measure the duration of a pulse on pin in microseconds, timing out after timeout microseconds (defaulting to 1 second) and returning 0 if the pulse does not transition or complete within the deadline.

tone generates a 50% duty cycle square wave of the specified frequency in Hertz on pin via mm.mcu PWM. If frequency is 0, tone acts as noTone. An optional duration in milliseconds specifies playback length; when duration > 0, tone expiration is evaluated during dispatch and terminates automatically once the deadline is reached. Durations exceeding half the unsigned long range are rejected with Status::BadArgument. Timed tones require an available slot in the concurrent tone table (up to 8 concurrent timed tones) and an operational clock; if no slot is available, Status::Busy is recorded without claiming or starting the output. noTone stops wave generation and releases the PWM output. Calling noTone on an idle pin is a safe no-op.

## Time

Opening fence
bool delay(unsigned long ms);
bool delayMicroseconds(unsigned int us);
[[nodiscard]] unsigned long millis();
[[nodiscard]] unsigned long micros();
Closing fence

delay suspends execution for the specified number of milliseconds. It executes in slices of at most 10 milliseconds, invoking dispatch between slices. If requestExit is asserted, delay aborts immediately and returns false without latching an error.

delayMicroseconds suspends execution for the specified number of microseconds via mm.mcu delay_us.

millis returns monotonic elapsed milliseconds since system boot via mm.mcu ticks_ms. On platforms lacking a hardware timer, millis returns 0 and records Status::Unsupported.

micros returns monotonic elapsed microseconds since system boot via mm.mcu ticks_us. On platforms lacking a hardware timer, micros returns 0 and records Status::Unsupported.

## Math and trigonometry

Opening fence
using std::abs;
using std::min;
using std::max;
using std::pow;
using std::sqrt;
using std::sin;
using std::cos;
using std::tan;

using std::asin;   using std::acos;   using std::atan;   using std::atan2;
using std::sinh;   using std::cosh;   using std::tanh;
using std::exp;    using std::log;    using std::log10;  using std::log2;
using std::cbrt;   using std::hypot;  using std::fabs;   using std::fmod;
using std::floor;  using std::ceil;   using std::round;  using std::trunc;
using std::isnan;  using std::isinf;  using std::isfinite; using std::signbit;

using std::fabsf;  using std::fmodf;  using std::powf;   using std::sqrtf;
using std::sinf;   using std::cosf;   using std::tanf;   using std::atan2f;
using std::expf;   using std::logf;   using std::floorf; using std::ceilf;
using std::roundf; using std::hypotf;

long sq(long x);
double sq(double x);

long constrain(long x, long a, long b);
double constrain(double x, double a, double b);

long map(long x, long in_min, long in_max, long out_min, long out_max);
double map(double x, double in_min, double in_max, double out_min, double out_max);
Closing fence

min, max, abs, pow, sqrt, sin, cos, and tan are exported in mm::sketch via using declarations of the standard library routines from <algorithm> and <cmath>, making them available unqualified in sketches.

The remaining names are exported the same way and for the same reason: a sketch reaches for round, hypot, fmod or atan2 without qualifying them, and a using declaration is what makes the standard routine answer to that spelling. Nothing is reimplemented, so each has the standard's behaviour, including its overloads for float, double, and long double. The float-suffixed C spellings are exported beside them because a sketch written for a machine without double-precision hardware writes sqrtf rather than sqrt, and the two are not interchangeable to a reader deciding what a line costs.

constrain, map, and sq are ordinary overloaded functions on long and double. Because they are functions rather than preprocessor macros, each argument is evaluated exactly once: an expression such as constrain(i++, 0, 9) increments i once, avoiding the multiple-evaluation defects of traditional macro definitions. If in_max equals in_min in map, the function returns out_min to prevent division by zero.

## Characters

Opening fence
bool isAlpha(int c);
bool isAlphaNumeric(int c);
bool isAscii(int c);
bool isControl(int c);
bool isDigit(int c);
bool isGraph(int c);
bool isHexadecimalDigit(int c);
bool isLowerCase(int c);
bool isPrintable(int c);
bool isPunct(int c);
bool isSpace(int c);
bool isUpperCase(int c);
bool isWhitespace(int c);
Closing fence

Character classification functions inspect the character argument c. Each function casts c to unsigned char before delegating to the corresponding <cctype> classification routine, ensuring well-defined behavior across all char values. isAscii returns true if c is in the range 0 through 127.

The semantic distinction between isSpace and isWhitespace is preserved: isSpace returns true for all whitespace characters including newline, carriage return, tab, and form feed (via std::isspace), whereas isWhitespace returns true only for blank space and horizontal tab characters (via std::isblank).

## Random numbers

Opening fence
long random(long max);
long random(long min, long max);
void randomSeed(unsigned long seed);
Closing fence

random generates pseudo-random long values using an internal std::minstd_rand engine initialized with a seed of 1. random(max) returns a value in the half-open interval [0, max); if max <= 0, it returns 0. random(min, max) returns a value in [min, max); if min >= max, it returns min.

randomSeed reseeds the internal engine with seed, or 1 if seed is 0, establishing a deterministic sequence.

## Bits and bytes

Opening fence
unsigned long bit(unsigned int n);

unsigned int bitRead(unsigned char x, unsigned int n);
unsigned int bitRead(unsigned int x, unsigned int n);
unsigned int bitRead(unsigned long x, unsigned int n);

void bitSet(unsigned char& x, unsigned int n);
void bitSet(unsigned int& x, unsigned int n);
void bitSet(unsigned long& x, unsigned int n);

void bitClear(unsigned char& x, unsigned int n);
void bitClear(unsigned int& x, unsigned int n);
void bitClear(unsigned long& x, unsigned int n);

void bitWrite(unsigned char& x, unsigned int n, byte b);
void bitWrite(unsigned int& x, unsigned int n, byte b);
void bitWrite(unsigned long& x, unsigned int n, byte b);

void bitWrite(unsigned char& x, unsigned int n, Level b);
void bitWrite(unsigned int& x, unsigned int n, Level b);
void bitWrite(unsigned long& x, unsigned int n, Level b);

byte lowByte(unsigned char x);
byte lowByte(unsigned int x);
byte lowByte(unsigned long x);

byte highByte(unsigned char x);
byte highByte(unsigned int x);
byte highByte(unsigned long x);
Closing fence

bit returns an unsigned long with bit n set (1UL << n), or 0 if n exceeds the bit width of unsigned long.

bitRead, bitSet, bitClear, bitWrite, lowByte, and highByte are provided as overload sets across the three unsigned integer widths: unsigned char, unsigned int, and unsigned long. bitSet, bitClear, and bitWrite take their first argument by non-const reference to mutate the target variable in place. Overloads for signed integer types (such as int and long) are explicitly deleted (= delete;), ensuring that applying these operations to signed variables produces a compile-time error rather than an implicit conversion.

## External interrupts

Opening fence
enum class Trigger {
    Rising,
    Falling,
    Change
};

inline constexpr Trigger RISING = Trigger::Rising;
inline constexpr Trigger FALLING = Trigger::Falling;
inline constexpr Trigger CHANGE = Trigger::Change;

bool attachInterrupt(int pin, void (*handler)(), Trigger trigger);
bool attachInterrupt(unsigned int pin, void (*handler)(), Trigger trigger);
bool detachInterrupt(int pin);
bool detachInterrupt(unsigned int pin);
[[nodiscard]] int digitalPinToInterrupt(int pin);
[[nodiscard]] unsigned int digitalPinToInterrupt(unsigned int pin);
Closing fence

attachInterrupt registers a handler function for GPIO edge events on the specified pin, configuring the pin for edge detection via mm.mcu. Trigger modes are RISING, FALLING, and CHANGE (mapped to mm::mcu::Edge::Rising, mm::mcu::Edge::Falling, and mm::mcu::Edge::Both). LOW and HIGH level interrupts are declined because level detection cannot be latched. The module maintains a fixed table of eight interrupt entries; attempting to attach a ninth returns false with Status::Busy. Re-attaching an already attached pin updates its handler and trigger, reusing the slot.

detachInterrupt clears the interrupt registration and unwatches the pin via mm.mcu gpio_unwatch.

digitalPinToInterrupt is the identity mapping: every GPIO pin that the latch supports serves directly as its own interrupt identifier.

The legacy interrupts and noInterrupts functions are declined. The modules.cpp execution model runs sketch handlers in thread context without preemption; disabling global interrupts is neither necessary nor supported.

## Communication

Opening fence
enum class Base {
    Dec = 10,
    Hex = 16,
    Oct = 8,
    Bin = 2
};

inline constexpr Base DEC = Base::Dec;
inline constexpr Base HEX = Base::Hex;
inline constexpr Base OCT = Base::Oct;
inline constexpr Base BIN = Base::Bin;

class SerialPort {
public:
    bool begin(unsigned long baud = 9600);
    bool end();

    std::size_t write(byte b);
    std::size_t write(const byte* buffer, std::size_t size);
    std::size_t write(const char* buffer, std::size_t size);
    std::size_t write(const char* s);

    std::size_t print(const char* s);
    std::size_t print(char c);
    std::size_t print(std::string_view s);
    std::size_t print(bool b);
    std::size_t print(int n, Base base = DEC);
    std::size_t print(unsigned int n, Base base = DEC);
    std::size_t print(long n, Base base = DEC);
    std::size_t print(unsigned long n, Base base = DEC);
    std::size_t print(double n, int digits = 2);

    std::size_t println(const char* s);
    std::size_t println(char c);
    std::size_t println(std::string_view s);
    std::size_t println(bool b);
    std::size_t println(int n, Base base = DEC);
    std::size_t println(unsigned int n, Base base = DEC);
    std::size_t println(long n, Base base = DEC);
    std::size_t println(unsigned long n, Base base = DEC);
    std::size_t println(double n, int digits = 2);
    std::size_t println();

    [[nodiscard]] int available();
    int read();
    int peek();
    bool flush();
    [[nodiscard]] bool connected();
    explicit operator bool();

    void setTimeout(unsigned long ms);
    [[nodiscard]] unsigned long getTimeout() const;

    std::size_t readBytes(char* buffer, std::size_t length);
    std::size_t readBytes(byte* buffer, std::size_t length);
    std::size_t readBytesUntil(char terminator, char* buffer, std::size_t length);
    std::size_t readBytesUntil(byte terminator, byte* buffer, std::size_t length);

    std::string readString();
    std::string readStringUntil(char terminator);

    bool find(const char* target);
    bool find(char target);
    bool findUntil(const char* target, const char* terminator);
    bool findUntil(const char* target, char terminator);

    long parseInt();
    double parseFloat();
};

extern SerialPort Serial;

void onSerial(void (*fn)());
Closing fence

Serial provides bidirectional serial communication over mm.stdio.

begin initializes the platform console; baud rate arguments (defaulting to 9600) are accepted for compatibility but ignored, as console transports manage baud rate at the platform layer. end is a no-op returning true. connected returns true if the console transport is connected to a host, and the explicit operator bool overload allows expressions such as while (!Serial);. flush flushes any buffered outgoing data to the underlying transport.

write outputs binary data or raw character buffers and returns the number of bytes written.

print and println format text, booleans, floating-point values, and integers across bases DEC (decimal), HEX (uppercase hexadecimal), OCT (octal), and BIN (binary) using std::to_chars into a stack buffer. println appends a carriage return and newline (\r\n).

available, read, and peek operate on an internal 64-byte receive ring buffer drained from the platform console during dispatch. available returns the count of unread bytes in the ring buffer. read returns the next byte from the ring or -1 if empty. peek returns the next byte without removing it or -1 if empty. When the underlying console read fails, available, read, and peek latch the error in the error latch and return their failure fallbacks.

setTimeout configures the read timeout in milliseconds (defaulting to 1000 milliseconds); getTimeout queries the configured timeout. Each waiting operation (readBytes, readBytesUntil, readString, readStringUntil, find, findUntil, parseInt, and parseFloat) establishes a single overall deadline and polls the receive ring and dispatch events until the requested condition is satisfied or the timeout expires. If requestExit is asserted during a waiting call, the operation aborts early and returns whatever count or partial result was accumulated.

find and findUntil perform streaming Knuth-Morris-Pratt pattern matching, supporting overlapping prefixes across stream slices without backtrack. parseInt parses signed integers into a signed long; values overflowing the representable range [LONG_MIN, LONG_MAX] are clamped to LONG_MAX or LONG_MIN and latch Status::BadArgument.

onSerial registers a callback invoked from dispatch once per pass whenever unread bytes remain in the receive ring buffer.

### SPI

Opening fence
enum class SpiMode { Mode0, Mode1, Mode2, Mode3 };

inline constexpr SpiMode SPI_MODE0 = SpiMode::Mode0;
inline constexpr SpiMode SPI_MODE1 = SpiMode::Mode1;
inline constexpr SpiMode SPI_MODE2 = SpiMode::Mode2;
inline constexpr SpiMode SPI_MODE3 = SpiMode::Mode3;

class SPISettings {
public:
    unsigned long clock = 4'000'000;
    BitOrder bit_order = BitOrder::MsbFirst;
    SpiMode data_mode = SpiMode::Mode0;

    constexpr SPISettings() = default;
    constexpr SPISettings(unsigned long clock_speed, BitOrder order, SpiMode mode);
};

class SPIClass {
public:
    bool begin();
    bool end();
    void beginTransaction(SPISettings settings);
    void endTransaction();
    byte transfer(byte val);
    word transfer16(word val);
    void transfer(void* buffer, std::size_t size);
    void transfer(byte* buffer, std::size_t size);
};

extern SPIClass SPI;
Closing fence

SPI provides SPI master communication over mm.mcu's spi_configure and spi_transfer.

begin initializes the SPI bus using the board's default SPI wiring (clock, transmit, and optional receive GPIOs) with default settings of 4 MHz, MSBFIRST, SPI_MODE0. If the board declares no SPI wiring, begin returns false and latches Status::Unsupported. end resets the sketch SPI state without deconfiguring the underlying peripheral hardware; the object may be re-initialized with begin.

beginTransaction applies a new SPISettings (clock speed, bit order, and SPI mode) by reconfiguring the underlying SPI peripheral via mm.mcu spi_configure. If begin has not been called, beginTransaction latches Status::NotInitialized. endTransaction is a no-op placeholder for symmetry.

transfer(byte) performs a full-duplex single-byte exchange and returns the received byte. transfer16(word) performs a two-byte full-duplex exchange, serializing and deserializing according to the current bit order. transfer(void*, size) and transfer(byte*, size) perform in-place full-duplex buffer exchanges: the buffer is overwritten with received data.

SPISettings bundles clock speed, bit order, and SPI mode. The three SPI mode constants (SPI_MODE0 through SPI_MODE3) map to mm::mcu::SpiMode::Mode0 through Mode3.

### Wire (I2C)

Opening fence
class TwoWire {
public:
    bool begin();
    bool end();
    void setClock(unsigned long clock_speed);

    void beginTransmission(byte address);
    void beginTransmission(int address);
    std::size_t write(byte val);
    std::size_t write(const byte* buffer, std::size_t size);
    std::size_t write(const char* s);
    byte endTransmission(bool send_stop = true);

    std::size_t requestFrom(byte address, std::size_t quantity, bool send_stop = true);
    std::size_t requestFrom(int address, int quantity, int send_stop = 1);

    [[nodiscard]] int available();
    int read();
    int peek();
    void flush();
};

extern TwoWire Wire;
Closing fence

Wire provides I2C master communication over mm.mcu's i2c_configure, i2c_write, i2c_read, and i2c_write_read.

begin initializes the I2C bus using the board's default I2C wiring (data and clock GPIOs) at 100 kHz. If the board declares no I2C wiring, begin returns false and latches Status::Unsupported. Wire.begin(addr) and the slave callbacks onReceive/onRequest are declined: mm.mcu is a master-only interface. end resets the sketch Wire state without deconfiguring the underlying peripheral hardware; the object may be re-initialized with begin.

setClock reconfigures the I2C clock speed. If begin has not been called, setClock latches Status::NotInitialized.

beginTransmission prepares a write transaction to the given seven-bit address. write buffers bytes into an internal thirty-two-byte transmit buffer. Writing beyond the buffer capacity returns 0 and sets an overflow flag. write(const char*) writes the string without its null terminator.

endTransmission executes the buffered write via mm.mcu i2c_write. It returns a status code: 0 on success, 1 if the transmit buffer overflowed, 2 on invalid argument (BadArgument, including an out-of-range address), 4 on other error (including transmission failures and NACKs), 5 on timeout. If send_stop is false, the write is deferred so that a subsequent requestFrom to the same address executes the combined write-then-read as a single mm.mcu i2c_write_read transaction, preserving bus arbitration.

requestFrom reads up to min(quantity, 32) bytes from the given address into an internal receive buffer. If a deferred write is pending for the same address, the operation becomes a combined write-then-read. Returns the number of bytes received.

available returns the count of unread bytes in the receive buffer. read returns the next byte and advances the read head, or -1 if the buffer is exhausted. peek returns the next byte without advancing the head, or -1. flush is a no-op.

## Variables, types, and constants

Opening fence
using byte = unsigned char;
